# The Oregon lottery: what randomization actually buys you
subtitle: "What a lottery makes true about the outcomes nobody sees, how to check that a draw was fair, and what Oregon's draw found in two years."
tags: ["randomization","balance table","medicaid","causal inference"]
teaches: ["methods/randomization","methods/balance-checks","concepts/selection-bias"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}
next: {"title":"The RAND Health Insurance Experiment: randomizing the price of care","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/the-rand-health-insurance-experiment-randomizing-the-price-of-care.cast"}

## What does a coin buy?
