    card title "What does a coin buy?"

A coin knows nothing about anyone's health. So why does a coin toss give (@draw r4_name r5_name r6_name parallel true@) a fair comparison, when a survey of a hundred thousand people does not?
    draw r1_name r2_name r3_name parallel true

To see why, take six people, made up to keep (@draw h_y1 h_y0@) the numbers small, and one outcome: visits to a doctor in a year.
    draw h_person rule_h

Each of them has two numbers: how o (@draw r1_y0 r2_y0 r3_y0 r4_y0 r5_y0 r6_y0@)ften they would go if covered, and how often if not.
    draw r1_y1 r2_y1 r3_y1 r4_y1 r5_y1 r6_y1

Ana would go three times with coverage, and never without. Fay, (@highlight r6_y1 r6_y0@) the sickest, nine times with it, or eight without.
    highlight r1_y1 r1_y0

The gap in each row is what coverage does for that person.
    draw h_t r1_t r2_t r3_t r4_t r5_t r6_t

Across all six, it adds t (@highlight avg_t effect circle@)wo visits a year: five against three.
    draw rule_a avg_lab avg_y1 avg_y0 avg_t

Now let them choose. The three who expect to need a doc (@highlight r4_d r5_d r6_d@)tor most, Dee, Eli and Fay, sign up.
    draw h_d r1_d r2_d r3_d r4_d r5_d r6_d

And the moment they choose, half the  (@draw r1_q r2_q r3_q r4_q r5_q r6_q parallel true@)table goes dark: we see the covered column for the (@draw r1_qt r2_qt r3_qt r4_qt r5_qt r6_qt parallel true@)m, and the other column for the rest.
    hide r1_y1 r2_y1 r3_y1 r4_y0 r5_y0 r6_y0 r1_t r2_t r3_t r4_t r5_t r6_t

    fade rule_a avg_lab avg_y1 avg_y0 avg_t to 0.2 duration 0.6

    quiz "Dee, Eli and Fay signed up. Covered average minus uncovered average: what will it come to?"
        intro: Before the sum, a prediction.
        right: Well above: the three who signed up would have gone to the doctor more even without coverage, so their numbers start high.
        wrong: Who signed up: the people who would go to the doctor most anyway, or least?
        * About two, the true effect
        * Well below two
        + Well above two

The covered average seven visits; the uncovered, one.
    draw seen_lab seen_y1 seen_y0

A gap of six, when coverage adds two (@highlight seen_gap avg_t effect circle@). Where do the other four come from?
    draw seen_gap

From the column we cannot see. Witho (@draw r4_g r5_g r6_g parallel true@)ut coverage, Dee, Eli and Fay would  (@draw su_lab su_y0@)have averaged five visits; the other three, one.
    hide r4_q r5_q r6_q

And coverage does for them what their own rows  (@draw r4_tg r5_tg r6_tg parallel true@)say: two, three and one visits, two on average  (@draw su_t@)for those who signed up.
    hide r4_qt r5_qt r6_qt

So the gap splits in two: the effect on those who signed up, plus how differently the two groups would have done without coverage.
    draw dec

Six is two plus four: the two from their own rows, and the four, five against one, is selection bias.
    draw dec_n

That two matches the average for all six only because the example was built that way; in general the two can differ.
    highlight su_t avg_t effect circle

And asking more people would not shrink the four at all: it lives in the column nobody sees.
    highlight dec part "{E[Y_i(0)\\mid D_i{=}1]-E[Y_i(0)\\mid D_i{=}0]}" effect underline

What we need is a way of choosing who is covered that cannot see either column, so that the uncovered can stand in for the covered.
    highlight seen_y0 effect circle

