    card title "A draw that cannot see"

So put the same six names in a hat and draw three. The hat cannot see their numbers; we can, only because we made them up.
    draw h_y1 h_y0 rule_h r1_name r1_y1 r1_y0 r2_name r2_y1 r2_y0 r3_name r3_y1 r3_y0 r4_name r4_y1 r4_y0 r5_name r5_y1 r5_y0 r6_name r6_y1 r6_y0

Say Cal, Dee and Eli are drawn: Z equals one for them.
    draw h_z r1_z r2_z r3_z r4_z r5_z r6_z

We would see the winners' visits with coverage, five  (@draw rule_a seen_lab seen_y1 seen_y0@)and a third on average, and the losers' visits without it, three.
    highlight r3_y1 r4_y1 r5_y1 r1_y0 r2_y0 r6_y0 effect circle

A gap of two and a third: near the true two, but not on it.
    draw axis tick_0 tick_2 tick_4 tick_6 tick_8 axis_cap true_line true_lab dot_first

Another draw gives another gap. Six names split three and three in twenty ways, and here are all twenty.
    draw dot_low dot_1 dot_2 dot_3 dot_4 dot_5 dot_6 dot_7 dot_8 dot_9 dot_10 dot_11 dot_12 dot_13 dot_14 dot_15 dot_17 dot_18 dot_high parallel true

Some draws are unlucky: if Ana, Ben and Cal win, the gap is minus two.
    highlight dot_low effect circle

And one draw in twenty hands the prize to exactly (@highlight dot_high effect circle@) Dee, Eli and Fay, and repeats the survey's six.
    draw high_lab

Yet the twenty gaps average exactly two, the true effect, with no tilt either way.
    highlight true_line true_lab

That is what the draw buys. Each person is as likely to land on one side as the other, so each side carries the  (@highlight true_line true_lab@)same mix of both columns, on average.
    draw ind

Both columns are balanced that way. Written out for the one we were missing: the visits without coverage have the same average among winners as among losers.
    draw mi

So the four visits of selectio (@highlight seen_y0 effect circle@)n bias, when people chose, become zero on average when a hat chooses.
    morph mi tex "E[{Y_i(0)}\\mid {Z_i}{=}1]-E[{Y_i(0)}\\mid {Z_i}{=}0]=0" duration 1.2

What it does not promise is any single draw: one draw can miss by four, either way. That is chance, and bigger groups shrink it.
    highlight dot_low dot_high effect circle

What it does promise reaches everything in the columns: motivation, family, a diagnosis nobody wrote down. Matching people on what you measured cannot promise that.
    highlight ind mi

    quiz "A clinic can send a nurse to half its diabetic patients. Which way of choosing them makes the two columns independent of the choice?" intro "A new case."
        right: The computer draw: it cannot see how anyone would fare, so both columns match on average in the two groups.
        wrong: For each one, ask: could whoever chooses see, or guess, how the patient would do?
        * The nurses pick those who need it most
        * Patients who ask for a visit get one
        + A computer draws half the names

With six made-up people we could check, because we invented the columns. With ninety thousand real ones, how would anyone know the draw was fair?
    highlight h_y1 h_y0

## Did the draw work?
voice: female
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein A, Taubman S, Wright B, Bernstein M, Gruber J, Newhouse JP, Allen H, Baicker K (Oregon Health Study Group), Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2012
      doi: 10.1093/qje/qjs020
      finding: '89,824 people signed up between 28 January and 29 February 2008 for about 10,000 places; eight draws, March to September 2008, selected 35,169. Study sample 74,922 (29,834 selected, 45,088 controls). Balance, full sample: nine lottery-list variables F = 1.29, p = 0.24; twelve pre-randomization hospital outcomes p = 0.84; both p = 0.56. Selected people were more often from two-person households (anyone listing another household member) (33.1% vs 16.4%), so all analyses control for household size.'

