    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["h_left","h_or","h_cr","h_cr2","rule_h","row1_lab","row1_or","row1_cr","row2_lab","row2_or","row2_cr","row3_lab","row3_or","row3_cr","rule_a","hh_lab","hh_or","hh_note","mark_or","src"] hidden true
    scratch list center cites ["finkelstein2012"] work ["Oregon, 2008","10,000 places","≈ 90,000 names","8 draws: ≈ 35,000 picked"] font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Did the draw work?"

Oregon ran a real draw of this kind, and everything it f (@draw list_line_1@)ound rests on that draw having been fair. So how would anyone check?
    draw list_box

It began in early 2008, w (@draw list_line_2@)hen the state had money to add about ten thousand low-income adults to Medicaid, and in five (@draw list_line_3@) weeks about ninety thousand put their names down.

Between March and September it drew names in eight rounds, about thirty-five thousand in  (@highlight list_line_4@)all, and only those drawn could apply.
    draw list_line_4

    erase list

The two columns are as hidden here as anywhere, so the check uses what was fixed before the draw, which winning could not change.
    draw h_left rule_h

First, nine answers from the sign-up form, like age, sex, language, and whether they signed up on the very first day. Then (@draw row2_lab@) twelve measures of hospital use from state records, for the months before.
    draw row1_lab

And one test of all of them together: do they, jointly, (@draw h_or src@) predict who was drawn?
    draw row3_lab

In Oregon they do not. The p-value is point  (@draw row1_or@)two four for the form, point eight f (@draw row2_or@)our for the hospital records, and point five six for all of t (@draw row3_or ends@)hem.

Differences that size turn up by chance alone a quarter of the time or more, even with a perfectly fair draw.
    draw mark_or

A crooked draw would look different: winners younger, fewer (@highlight row1_lab row2_lab@) past hospital stays, quicker to sign up, every gap leaning the same way.
    draw h_cr h_cr2

Each joint test would then fall below one in a thousand: whatever sorted the two groups, it was not chance.
    draw row1_cr row2_cr row3_cr parallel true

    quiz "Suppose clerks quietly let people who phoned in on the first day jump the queue. Which row flags it?"
        intro: Which row would catch it?
        right: The sign-up form: signing up on the first day is one of its nine answers, and winners would have far more of it.
        wrong: Look for the row that records when people signed up.
        + The sign-up form
        * Hospital use
        * Neither: only the outcomes would show it

One row in Oregon is out of balance on purpose.
    draw rule_a hh_lab

The state drew people, but a winner's whole household could apply, so anyone w (@draw hh_note@)ho listed another household member held two tickets.
    draw hh_or

Winners came from two-person households twice as often, so every Oregon estimate compares people within the same household size.
    highlight hh_or

The check passes. But it checked only what can be seen: the hidden columns are trusted to the lottery, never proved by the table.
    highlight h_left

## Twenty rows, one star
voice: female
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation: y = 100*(1 - 0.95^x)
    params:
      'n': {value: 1, min: 1, max: 40, step: 1, label: rows}
    x_range:
      - 1
      - 40
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 100
    show_equation: false
    x_label: rows tested, each at 5 %
    y_label: chance of a star (%)
    marks:
      - {kind: point, at: 'n', curve: 0, label: true, id: you}
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein et al. (Oregon Health Study Group), Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2012
      doi: 10.1093/qje/qjs020
      finding: Survey response 50.6% among controls; selected people 1.6 percentage points less likely to respond (SE 0.7). Among respondents, treatment and control remain balanced (all joint p-values at least 0.28). In the RAND experiment, response was 87% on the most generous plan against 63% on the least (citing Newhouse et al. 1993).

    label lab_curve "fair draw" below #8a5fa8 attach_to curve_0 font_size 34 hidden true
    path rel smooth dashed thick #8f887c points [[1,2.53],[2,5],[3,7.41],[4,9.75],[5,12.04],[6,14.26],[7,16.43],[8,18.55],[9,20.61],[10,22.62],[11,24.58],[12,26.49],[13,28.35],[14,30.17],[15,31.93],[16,33.66],[17,35.34],[18,36.98],[19,38.57],[20,40.13],[21,41.64],[22,43.12],[23,44.56],[24,45.96],[25,47.33],[26,48.67],[27,49.97],[28,51.23],[29,52.47],[30,53.67],[31,54.84],[32,55.99],[33,57.1],[34,58.19],[35,59.25],[36,60.28],[37,61.28],[38,62.26],[39,63.22],[40,64.15]] data true hidden true
    text lab_rel "related rows" #8f887c at.data [33,45] font_size 34 hidden true
    scratch att center cites ["finkelstein2012"] work ["Answered: more cover vs less","Oregon: 49.0 % vs 50.6 %","RAND: 87 % vs 63 %","Finkelstein et al. 2012"] font_size 46 hidden true

    card title "Twenty rows, one star"

Why one joint test, rather than reading the rows of a balance table one at a time?
    draw axes

Because a fair draw still knocks the odd row out of line. Tested on its own at the usual five percent level, a row earns a star one time in twenty.
    draw curve_0 lab_curve you

Across: how many rows are tested. Up: the chance that at le (@highlight y_label@)ast one gets a star, if the rows were unrelated.
    highlight x_label

Move the point to the right, to more rows, and watch the chance of a false star climb.
    explore params ["n"] watch "Watch the point slide to more rows, and the chance of a false star climb."

With Oregon's nine sign-up answers, it is already over a third.
    animate params.n.value 9 duration 2.5

With twenty rows, almost two in three. A perfectly fair lottery usually shows a star somewhere.
    animate params.n.value 20 duration 2.5

Real rows are related, since age and hospital stays move together, so the true chance is lower, here drawn for illustration. But a star or two is still what fairness looks like.
    draw rel lab_rel

So a lone star is no alarm. A pattern is: many rows leaning the same way, and that is what the joint test weighs.
    highlight curve_0

    quiz "A trial's balance table has thirty rows. Two are starred, one tilted each way, and the joint test gives p = 0.4. What do you conclude?" intro "A new table."
        right: Chance: thirty fair rows earn one or two stars on average, and a joint p of 0.4 finds no pattern.
        wrong: How many stars would thirty rows from a fair draw show, on average?
        * The randomization failed
        + Nothing alarming: chance
        * Drop the two starred rows and rerun

    erase curve_0 rel lab_curve lab_rel you axes

    card title "Who stays"

The alarm that does ring in real trials comes after the draw: who stays to be measured.
    draw att_box att_line_1

Oregon's winners  (@draw att_line_2@)were 1.6 points less likely to answer its mail survey, 49 percent against about 51: detectable, but small, and the people who did answer were still balanced.

In the RAND insurance experiment of the 1970s, 87 percent of those on the most generous plan responded, against 63 p (@draw att_line_4@)ercent on the least generous.
    draw att_line_3

A gap like that can undo a perfect draw, because the people who leave are not chosen by chance.
    highlight att_line_3

## What two years of coverage changed
voice: female
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein et al. (Oregon Health Study Group), Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2012
      doi: 10.1093/qje/qjs020
      finding: Winning the lottery raised Medicaid coverage by about 25 percentage points. In the first year, coverage raised the probability of an outpatient visit by 21 percentage points (35%) and lowered the probability of an unpaid medical bill sent to collection by 6.4 points (25%). About 14% of those not drawn were on Medicaid at some point anyway.
    - id: baicker2013
      title: 'The Oregon Experiment: Effects of Medicaid on Clinical Outcomes'
      authors: Baicker et al. (Oregon Health Study Group), New England Journal of Medicine
      year: 2013
      doi: 10.1056/NEJMsa1212321
      finding: '12,229 people interviewed and examined in the Portland area, 2009–10, on average 25 months after the lottery. IV effects of Medicaid coverage (95% CI): office visits +2.70 a year (0.91 to 4.49; control 5.5); positive depression screen −9.15 points (−16.70 to −1.60; control 30.0%); catastrophic out-of-pocket spending (over 30% of income) −4.48 points (−8.26 to −0.69; control 5.5%); systolic blood pressure −0.52 mm Hg (−2.97 to 1.93); glycated haemoglobin ≥ 6.5% −0.93 points (−4.44 to 2.59; control 5.1%); total cholesterol +2.20 mg/dl (−3.44 to 7.84); diabetes diagnosis +3.83 points (1.93 to 5.73; control 1.1%); diabetes medication +5.43 points.'
takeaways:
    - A coin cannot see the hidden columns
    - Check balance jointly, and who stays
    - No detected change is not no change

