    card title "What two years of coverage changed"

So, with a draw that passed its checks, what did coverage actually do in Oregon's first two years?
    draw y1_box y1_line_1

Winning raised the chance of being on Medicaid by about twenty-five points; some of those not drawn, about one in seven, got on anyway.
    draw y1_line_2

So each gap between winners and losers is divided by that twenty-five-point jump. If winning mattered only through coverage, that gives the effect of coverage for the people the lottery put on Medicaid.
    highlight y1_line_2

In the first year, the covered were about a third more likely to have an outpatient visit, and  (@draw y1_line_4@)a quarter less likely to have a medical bill sent to collectors.
    draw y1_line_3

    erase y1

About two years in, researchers interviewed and examined some twe (@draw src@)lve thousand people in and around Portland.
    draw h_out h_wo h_eff rule_h

Doctor visits: five and a half a year among those not drawn, and about 2.7 more with coverage, somewhere between one and four and a half.
    draw vis_lab vis_wo vis_eff vis_ci

Screening positive for depression: thirty percent among those not drawn, and about nine points fewer with coverage, a range from under two to almost seventeen.
    draw dep_lab dep_wo dep_eff dep_ci

Out-of-pocket bills above thirty percent of income: five and a half percent, and nearly gone with coverage; this range, too, stays clear of zero.
    draw bil_lab bil_wo bil_eff bil_ci

    quiz "Blood pressure, cholesterol, blood sugar: what did two years of coverage do to them?" intro "A guess first." judge false
        right: No change the study could detect: each estimate sits near zero, with a range that runs past it both ways.
        * Clearly better
        * No change the study could detect
        * Clearly worse

Blood pressure, blood sugar and cholesterol: no change the study could detect, and every range runs past zero both ways.
    draw rule_m bp_lab bp_wo bp_eff bp_ci sug_lab sug_wo sug_eff sug_ci cho_lab cho_wo cho_eff cho_ci

But no detected change is not the same as no change. Blood pressure's range runs from about three points lower to two higher.
    highlight bp_ci

And here economists read the same rows differently.
    highlight sug_lab

Some take them as evidence that two years of coverage did little for these markers.
    highlight sug_eff sug_ci

Others note that only about one person in twenty had high blood sugar at all, so the range is too wide to rule out a worthwhile effect.
    highlight sug_wo effect circle

What would tell them apart is many more people with diabetes in the study, or a longer wait.
    highlight sug_ci

Coverage did find more diabetes, though: new diagnoses up nearly four points, and diabetes medication up five.
    draw rule_d dia_lab dia_wo dia_eff dia_ci

    quiz "A city gives free bus passes by lottery to 2,000 of 6,000 applicants. The draw balances, and a year on, winners report more job interviews. What can you claim?" intro "One new case."
        right: Among people who applied, the offer of a pass raised interviews. The draw settles that, and nothing wider.
        wrong: Who was in the draw, and what exactly did the draw hand out?
        * Bus passes raise interviews for everyone in the city
        + The offer of a pass raised interviews among applicants like these
        * Nothing, since winners chose whether to use their passes

    clear

So what does a coin buy? Not two identical groups, but a choice that cannot see the hidden columns, so they match on average on both sides.
    draw takeaway_1

That cannot be tested directly. What can be checked is everything fixed before the draw, all together, and who stays to be measured.
    draw takeaway_2

And the results are read by their ranges: in two years, Oregon's coin moved care, money and mood, and left the body's markers an open question.
    draw takeaway_3

Six people or ninety thousand, the coin's one virtue is the same: it knows nothing about anyone.
    highlight takeaway_1

